---
/**
 * /about — A2 "Named-role sections", Figma `238:101` on page
 * `Page — About (audience-first)` (`238:98`), file `wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU`.
 * Motion: `Motion — About page` (`245:2`, frame `245:3`).
 *
 * WHY A2 AND NOT A PORT. The owner's brief was "adopt similar messaging like
 * whom we build this for". A2 is the most literal answer available: it names
 * four readers, gives them four chips that double as a page map, and then
 * addresses one section to each of them, every section carrying a quantified
 * artefact cited to the statute that binds THAT reader. Cover the prose and the
 * four artefacts still make the argument, which is the test in
 * specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 3.
 *
 * NAME COLLISION, recorded so nobody re-derives it: a different Figma page
 * `Page — About` (`106:2`) holds two earlier section studies also labelled A1
 * and A2. Those are NOT this design.
 *
 * ── WHAT SURVIVED FROM THE 2026-08-02 COPY PASS, AND WHY EACH ONE MATTERS ───
 *
 * 1. "Bid practitioners", never "engineers" and specifically never
 *    "consultants". The Vision below promises a supplier can answer a tender
 *    "without paying a bid-writing consultancy"; naming ourselves as the thing
 *    the reader is avoiding is an own goal. The word survives in the hero
 *    standfirst and as the heading of the provenance section.
 * 2. The founders' 2010 record appears twice, and BOTH times the same paragraph
 *    says it is their record before CrowAgent and not a result this software
 *    produced. No customer, client or project is named or implied.
 * 3. Values states the refusal outright: we do not quote win rates, ours or
 *    yours. Refusing that claim is the position, and a position has to be
 *    stated to count.
 * 4. Market-neutral framing. The eyebrow no longer leads with jurisdiction and
 *    neither Mission nor Vision is scoped to the UK. UK public is the proof
 *    point, not the identity.
 * 5. The timeline marks its unshipped item "Planned, not yet built" as TEXT.
 *    The legacy page carried that state in `opacity-50` alone, so read by
 *    anyone who cannot see the dimming a planned feature read exactly like a
 *    delivered one.
 * 6. Company facts exact, and the trust wording verbatim from data/footer.ts,
 *    including "We follow ISO 27001 controls. We are not certified yet." We
 *    hold neither SOC 2 nor ISO 27001 and must not imply otherwise.
 *
 * ── THREE DELIBERATE DEPARTURES FROM THE RENDER, EACH ARGUED ────────────────
 *
 * A. THE MARK COLOURS. The render paints the four artefact dots teal, cyan,
 *    orchid, teal. The binding palette rule (PLATFORM-CHARTER.md, and
 *    DESIGN-DECISIONS.md "Colour carries meaning") is teal = verified,
 *    violet/orchid = refused or flagged, cyan = INTERACTIVE ONLY, and it warns
 *    in terms that using all three decoratively is the generic-AI-startup
 *    palette. Read semantically, three of the four artefacts are verified facts
 *    cited to statute and exactly one is the refusal rule ("Every figure, or an
 *    open gap"). So the marks are three teal and one orchid, on the refusal.
 *    That is also the same grammar the trust row uses five rows later, five
 *    teal and one orchid on the thing we do not hold, so the page teaches the
 *    mark once and reuses it rather than spending three hues on decoration.
 *
 * B. THE STATUTE ATTRIBUTION IN ROLE 1. The render's paragraph reads "under
 *    s.71 you assess and publish against them". s.52 and s.71 are duties on the
 *    CONTRACTING AUTHORITY, not on the supplier the section is addressed to,
 *    which is a distinction this page already records under CrowMark for
 *    Suppliers. The sentence names the authority as the duty holder instead.
 *    The two duties stay separate everywhere, as they must.
 *
 * C. THE CLOSING SECOND CTA. The render's pair is "Book a 30-minute call" and
 *    "Send a question". The legacy page's closing pair was the call and the
 *    free PPN 002 calculator, and the owner approved keeping the calculator.
 *    The hero keeps the designed pair, so "Send a question" is still on the
 *    page and /contact is still one click away; the close carries the tool.
 *
 * ── MOTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Three of the five sequences on `245:3` have elements in A2 and are built:
 * M-A1 (hero blooms), M-A3 (role chips), M-A5 (trust dots). M-A2 (a divider
 * drawing down the page) and M-A4 (branch arms, which self-scope to "A6 hero
 * only") have NO corresponding node anywhere in `238:101` — the frame's whole
 * child list is a hero, four role frames, a trust frame and a close frame, and
 * none contains a rule or a stem. Inventing them would be inventing design.
 *
 * Everything here is CSS. M-A5 is specified with an IntersectionObserver, and
 * the build ships 0 KB of JavaScript; a page-scoped observer would be exactly
 * the "one-off JavaScript" PLATFORM-CHARTER.md forbids. A named view timeline
 * does the same job with no script, behind `@supports`, with the final state as
 * the fallback, which is what DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 3 requires of
 * `animation-timeline`. The trade is honest and is recorded in the report: a
 * scroll timeline is scrubbed rather than played once, so scrolling back up
 * replays it.
 *
 * Every sequence sits inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, so the
 * reduced-motion state is not an override that has to win an argument with the
 * cascade. It is the absence of any animation at all, with every element
 * already at its final state.
 *
 * ── SEO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The description changed, owner-approved. It read "A London-based team that
 * reads the UK procurement rules", which contradicts the market-neutral
 * decision the rest of this page was corrected to (OA-25). check-seo-parity.js
 * fails on a field that is MISSING, not on one that changed, and /about is not
 * among the routes tests/parity.spec.js discovers — that spec walks blog/,
 * compare/, sectors/ and glossary/ only — so there is no baseline to move.
 * Title and canonical are the legacy values, untouched.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import NewsletterForm from '../components/forms/NewsletterForm.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'About Us | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'Written for bid teams and readable by the people scoring them. CrowAgent Ltd is built by bid practitioners: company details, history, what we stand for and the statutes we build against.';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/crowagent-platform/30min';
const COMPANIES_HOUSE =
  'https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/17076461';
const PPN_CALCULATOR = '/tools/ppn-002-calculator';
const CONTACT = '/contact#contact-form';

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
  ]),
];

/**
 * THE FOUR READERS. `mark` is the semantic colour of the artefact the section
 * carries, not a per-section accent — see departure A in the header note.
 * `verified` is a fact cited to a named source; `refused` is the engine
 * declining to write a figure it cannot trace.
 */
type Mark = 'verified' | 'refused';
const ROLES: {
  id: string;
  chip: string;
  eyebrow: string;
  heading: string;
  body: string;
  figure: string;
  source: string;
  mark: Mark;
  /** The artefact sits left and the prose right, alternating down the page. */
  mirror: boolean;
}[] = [
  {
    id: 'role-run',
    chip: 'If you run the bid',
    eyebrow: 'If you run the bid',
    heading: 'You need the answer to survive a year of scrutiny',
    // Departure B. The render put the s.71 duty on "you"; it is the authority's.
    body: 'Everything you price in the response becomes a promise. Under s.52 the authority sets and publishes at least three KPIs on a contract over £5m, and under s.71 it assesses and publishes performance against them at least every twelve months. CrowMark drafts with that in view: every claim names the source it came from, and a claim with no recorded commitment behind it is refused rather than written.',
    figure: '3 KPIs minimum, published at award',
    source: 'Procurement Act 2023, s.52',
    mark: 'verified',
    mirror: false,
  },
  {
    id: 'role-sign',
    chip: 'If you sign it off',
    eyebrow: 'If you sign it off',
    heading: 'You are the one who has to stand behind the numbers',
    body: 'A confident paragraph is not evidence. Before you sign, you can see which figures trace back to a recorded commitment and which do not, because the ones that do not are listed rather than blended in. That is a shorter review, and a defensible one.',
    figure: 'Every figure, or an open gap',
    // The only artefact on the page that is not a cited figure, because it is
    // not a figure: it is the rule the other three are produced under.
    source: 'No figure without a source',
    mark: 'refused',
    mirror: true,
  },
  {
    id: 'role-score',
    chip: 'If you score it',
    eyebrow: 'If you score it',
    heading: 'You are deciding whether to believe a stranger',
    body: 'The same engine that writes answers reads them. Claims arrive with their citations attached, so you check the sentence against the clause instead of trusting the tone. Where a claim has no source, that is visible to you rather than hidden by fluent prose.',
    figure: '10% minimum evaluation weighting',
    source: 'PPN 002, mandatory 1 October 2025',
    mark: 'verified',
    mirror: false,
  },
  {
    id: 'role-deliver',
    chip: 'If you have to deliver it',
    eyebrow: 'If you have to deliver it',
    heading: 'You inherit somebody else’s promises',
    body: 'The commitments made at bid stage become KPIs you are measured on. CrowMark keeps each one attached to the evidence that proves it, month by month, so the twelve-month assessment is assembled from a record rather than reconstructed from memory.',
    figure: '12 months to the assessment',
    source: 'Procurement Act 2023, s.71',
    mark: 'verified',
    mirror: true,
  },
];

/**
 * Two cards. The old pair was "The approach" and "What you get", and they said
 * the same thing twice: both opened on starting from the actual regulation
 * rather than a template. That freed one card for the substantiation the
 * heading needs, and the duplication is merged into the other.
 */
const APPROACH = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'Who builds it',
    body: 'The founders have been writing and winning bids since 2010, including multi-million pound contracts taken from tender through to delivery. That is their own record before CrowAgent existed, not a result this software produced. What that work teaches is narrower and more useful than any headline: most marks are lost where the evidence already exists and nobody can point to it.',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'How it is built',
    body: 'Every feature starts from the source rule rather than a template. PPN 002 for social value, and the two separate KPI duties in the Procurement Act 2023: s.52 sets and publishes at least 3 KPIs on contracts over £5m, and s.71 assesses performance against them. Each answer carries the clause it was written against, and every figure is checked against your own confirmed data before it is shown, or the draft is rejected.',
  },
];

/**
 * Mission and Vision were both scoped to the UK, which contradicts the
 * market-neutral narrative. UK public is kept as the proof point, in Mission
 * only, so the same sentence is not made twice.
 *
 * Values carries the refusal explicitly. The register to define against
 * advertises a "241% increase in success rates"; not making that class of claim
 * is a position, and a position has to be stated to count.
 */
const PRINCIPLES = [
  {
    kicker: 'Mission',
    heading: 'Make bidding answerable, whatever your size',
    body: 'Any supplier should be able to answer a requirement from evidence it already holds, whatever its size or budget, with figures the software cannot invent and a named person on every approval. UK public procurement is where we prove it, because its duties are published.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'Vision',
    heading: 'Answers you can defend, first time',
    body: 'A market where any supplier can answer a tender requirement from evidence they already hold, cited to source, without paying a bid-writing consultancy to assemble it.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'Values',
    heading: 'Statute over speculation',
    body: 'Every figure cites its source. A figure that cannot be traced to your own confirmed data is rejected rather than shown, nothing is rounded into place to fill a gap, and we do not quote win rates, ours or yours.',
  },
];

/** `status` is rendered as text, never as opacity alone. See the note above. */
type Status = 'done' | 'now' | 'planned';
const TIMELINE: {
  when: string;
  heading: string;
  body: string;
  status: Status;
  link?: { label: string; href: string };
}[] = [
  {
    // 2010, not 2021. This is the dated form of the fact, and the second
    // sentence is load-bearing: it is the founders' record, not CrowAgent's,
    // and it names nobody because there is nobody to name.
    when: '2010 to 2025',
    heading: 'Founding team experience',
    body: 'Bid and tender work inside regulated industries from 2010, including multi-million pound bids written, won and delivered to completion. That is the founders’ own record before CrowAgent, not a result of the product. It is also where we saw at first hand how much of the friction smaller suppliers face is evidence that exists and cannot be found.',
    status: 'done',
  },
  {
    when: 'Jan to Feb 2026',
    heading: 'Regulatory research',
    body: 'Deep dive into the statutory frameworks: PPN 002, published 13 February 2025 and mandatory from 1 October 2025, and the KPI duties in the Procurement Act 2023 at s.52 and s.71. Every figure has to trace back to a named, citeable source.',
    status: 'done',
  },
  {
    when: 'March 2026',
    heading: 'CrowAgent founded',
    body: 'Incorporated as CrowAgent Ltd in England and Wales (Company No. 17076461). Engine prototyping begins.',
    status: 'done',
    link: { label: 'Company 17076461 on Companies House', href: COMPANIES_HOUSE },
  },
  {
    when: 'May 2026',
    heading: 'Platform live',
    body: 'CrowMark in live production.',
    status: 'now',
  },
  {
    when: 'Q4 2026',
    heading: 'Regulatory monitor',
    // The word "Live" opened this sentence while the chip beside it said the
    // item is not built. Two signals, one of them wrong. The tense agrees with
    // the status now.
    body: 'A regulatory change feed for UK public procurement, surfacing material updates straight into your dashboard once it ships.',
    status: 'planned',
  },
];

const STATUS_LABEL: Record<Status, string> = {
  done: 'Delivered',
  now: 'Live now',
  planned: 'Planned, not yet built',
};

const COMPANY = [
  { label: 'Registered name', value: 'CrowAgent Ltd' },
  { label: 'Registered in', value: 'England and Wales' },
  { label: 'Company number', value: '17076461', mono: true },
  { label: 'Data protection', value: 'ICO registered data controller' },
  { label: 'Data residency', value: 'UK and EU' },
  { label: 'Founded', value: 'March 2026' },
];

const VARIANTS = [
  {
    heading: 'CrowMark for Suppliers',
    // s.52 and s.71 are duties on the AUTHORITY, so a supplier feature cannot
    // be described as publishing or assessing under either. What a supplier
    // does is produce the evidence, which is what this says.
    body: 'Tender discovery from Contracts Finder and Find a Tender, the 10% minimum social-value weighting PPN 002 sets on in-scope central government contracts, PPN 017 AI disclosure, and, after award, evidence for the KPIs an authority sets under s.52 and assesses under s.71.',
    href: '/crowmark',
    cta: 'See the supplier side',
  },
  {
    heading: 'CrowMark for Buyers',
    body: 'Publish your requirements against a deterministic social value rubric, then locate the evidence for each one across the responses you receive, quoted verbatim or dropped. CrowMark organises; your evaluation panel scores.',
    href: '/crowmark-buyers',
    cta: 'See the buyer side',
  },
];

/**
 * TABLE STAKES. Six rows, and the sixth is the reason the section exists: it
 * says what we do NOT hold. The wording of that row is verbatim from
 * data/footer.ts `trustNote`, minus the asterisk that footnote syntax needs and
 * a card does not. We hold neither SOC 2 nor ISO 27001 certification.
 */
const TRUST: { term: string; detail: string; mark: Mark }[] = [
  { term: 'AES-256 at rest', detail: 'Encrypted where it is stored.', mark: 'verified' },
  { term: 'TLS 1.3 in transit', detail: 'Encrypted while it moves.', mark: 'verified' },
  {
    term: 'UK and EU data residency',
    detail: 'Your documents stay in the region.',
    mark: 'verified',
  },
  {
    term: 'GDPR compliant',
    detail: 'Lawful basis, retention and deletion documented.',
    mark: 'verified',
  },
  { term: 'ICO registered', detail: 'Registered with the UK regulator.', mark: 'verified' },
  {
    term: 'ISO 27001 controls',
    detail: 'We follow ISO 27001 controls. We are not certified yet.',
    mark: 'refused',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/about" schema={schema}>
  {/*
    HERO. `Section` owns the measure, the padding, the centred head block and
    the gradient capsule eyebrow, so nothing below restates any of them. The
    wrapper exists only to give the two blooms a positioned, isolated box to
    paint inside, underneath the content layer.
  */}
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="hero__mesh" aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="hero__b1"></b>
      <b class="hero__b2"></b>
    </div>

    <Section
      eyebrow="Who we build this for"
      title="Written for bid teams. Readable by the people scoring them."
      standfirst="CrowAgent Ltd is a UK company built by bid practitioners. Four people touch a tender and want four different things from it. The sections below are addressed to each of them in turn."
      id="about-hero"
      as="h1"
    >
      {/*
        The chips are the page map, so they are links rather than labels. That
        is also why they are 44px tall: a control that navigates has to be a
        target, and 44px is the WCAG 2.2 floor this repo holds itself to.
      */}
      <nav class="chips" aria-label="The four readers this page addresses">
        <ul class="chips__list">
          {
            ROLES.map((r) => (
              <li>
                <a class="chip sv-hit" href={`#${r.id}`}>
                  <span class:list={['chip__dot', `chip__dot--${r.mark}`]} aria-hidden="true" />
                  {r.chip}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <div class="cta-row">
        <Button href={CALENDLY} target="_blank">Book a 30-minute call</Button>
        <Button href={CONTACT} variant="secondary">Send a question</Button>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </div>

  {/*
    THE FOUR ROLE SECTIONS. These are the one place on the page that does NOT
    use Section's head block, and deliberately: the design addresses each reader
    directly in a left-anchored editorial column with the artefact beside it,
    and a centred eyebrow-heading-standfirst stack would break the address. The
    centring decision is scoped to the head block and to card content, and this
    is neither. Section still owns the measure, the gutter and the padding.
  */}
  {
    ROLES.map((r) => (
      <Section id={r.id}>
        {/*
          THE MIRROR MODIFIER GOES ON THE GRID, NOT ON THE SECTION, and that is
          not a style preference. Astro scopes a component's CSS with its own
          cid, and `Section`'s root <section> carries SECTION's cid rather than
          this page's, so a `.role--mirror` written here and passed down as a
          class prop compiles to a selector that can never match the element it
          was written for. It rendered in the class list and did nothing.
        */}
        <div class:list={['role__grid', { 'role__grid--mirror': r.mirror }]}>
          <div class="role__text">
            <p class:list={['role__eyebrow', `role__eyebrow--${r.mark}`]}>{r.eyebrow}</p>
            <h2 class="role__h">{r.heading}</h2>
            <p class="role__p">{r.body}</p>
          </div>

          {/*
            The artefact is a statement and the source that binds it, which is a
            term and its definition, so it is a <dl>. That is also why it stays
            left: surfaces.css excludes a card holding a list from centring
            automatically, "because a list is read down a left edge". Recorded
            here rather than left to look like a coincidence — the design shows
            it left, and the structural rule is the honest way to get there
            instead of a manual opt-out with no argument behind it.
          */}
          <div class="surface surface--pad role__art">
            <dl class:list={['art', `art--${r.mark}`]}>
              <dt class="art__figure">{r.figure}</dt>
              <dd class="art__source">{r.source}</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Section>
    ))
  }

  <Section
    eyebrow="Who is behind it"
    title="Built by bid practitioners who read the rules."
    standfirst="CrowMark starts from the rule that applies and works it the same way every time: find the work, draft answers cited to source, evidence delivery after award. UK public procurement is the worked example because its duties are published."
    id="practitioners"
  >
    <div class="pair">
      {
        APPROACH.map((a) => (
          <div class="pair__card surface surface--pad surface--read">
            <p class="kicker">{a.eyebrow}</p>
            <p class="pair__body">{a.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Mission, vision, values" title="What we stand for." id="principles">
    <ul class="principles">
      {
        PRINCIPLES.map((p) => (
          <li class="principles__item">
            <p class="kicker">{p.kicker}</p>
            <h3 class="principles__h">{p.heading}</h3>
            <p class="principles__body">{p.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="History" title="Where we are." id="timeline">
    <ol class="tl">
      {
        TIMELINE.map((t) => (
          <li class:list={['tl__item', `tl__item--${t.status}`]}>
            <p class="tl__when">{t.when}</p>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <h3 class="tl__h">
                {t.heading}
                {/* Text, not opacity. A planned item must not read as delivered. */}
                <span class:list={['tl__status', `tl__status--${t.status}`]}>
                  {STATUS_LABEL[t.status]}
                </span>
              </h3>
              <p class="tl__p">{t.body}</p>
              {t.link && (
                <p class="tl__p">
                  <a href={t.link.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    {t.link.label}
                  </a>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Company details" title="CrowAgent Ltd" id="company">
    <dl class="company">
      {
        COMPANY.map((c) => (
          <div class="company__row">
            <dt>{c.label}</dt>
            <dd class={c.mono ? 'is-mono' : undefined}>{c.value}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <div class="company__row">
        <dt>Contact</dt>
        <dd><a href="mailto:hello@crowagent.ai">hello@crowagent.ai</a></dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The stack"
    title="One product, two variants."
    standfirst="CrowMark serves both sides of a procurement, and both work in the public sector and the private sector."
    id="variants"
  >
    <div class="pair">
      {
        VARIANTS.map((v) => (
          <div class="pair__card surface surface--pad surface--read">
            <h3 class="pair__h">{v.heading}</h3>
            <p class="pair__body">{v.body}</p>
            <p class="pair__body">
              <a href={v.href}>{v.cta}</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    TABLE STAKES. Each card holds a <dl>, which is the same construct the four
    artefact cards use and for the same two reasons: a term and what it means IS
    a description list, and surfaces.css then keeps the card left-aligned by its
    own structural rule rather than by an override somebody has to argue for.
    One mechanism, used twice on one page.
  */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Table stakes"
    title="Secure, resident in the UK, and honest about what we do not hold."
    id="trust"
  >
    <div class="trust">
      {
        TRUST.map((t) => (
          <div class:list={['surface', 'surface--pad', 'trust__card', `trust__card--${t.mark}`]}>
            <dl class="trust__pair">
              <dt class="trust__term">{t.term}</dt>
              <dd class="trust__detail">{t.detail}</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    CLOSE — mirrors the hero, and repeats its eyebrow on purpose. The second
    button is the free PPN 002 calculator, which the legacy page closed with and
    which the owner approved keeping; the standfirst names what the buttons
    actually do rather than promising a third thing.
  */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Who we build this for"
    title="Whichever of the four you are."
    standfirst="Book a 30-minute call, or run the free PPN 002 calculator now. No card, no signup."
    id="about-cta"
  >
    <div class="cta-row">
      <Button href={CALENDLY} target="_blank">Book a 30-minute call</Button>
      <Button href={PPN_CALCULATOR} variant="secondary">Try the PPN 002 calculator (free)</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    NEWSLETTER — restored from about.html:422-433, which the first port dropped.
    It is the page's only lead capture that is not a call booking, so losing it
    was a commercial regression rather than a cosmetic one. Heading and
    standfirst are the legacy <h2> and its description verbatim.
  */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Monthly digest"
    title="Get monthly UK procurement digests"
    standfirst="PPN 002 social value, tender-market trends and CrowMark product news, once a month. Unsubscribe any time."
    id="newsletter"
  >
    <NewsletterForm id="about-nl" source="about-newsletter" />
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* ══ HERO ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     The two blooms of Figma `240:3` and `240:4`. Same rig as FinalCta and
     HeroStack: absolutely positioned discs painting --orb-teal and
     --orb-violet through `mix-blend-mode: screen`, because normal blending
     would paint two grey discs onto a dark page. They sit at z-index -1 inside
     an isolated context, so no light lands on the surface a word sits on. */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  .hero__mesh {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__b1,
  .hero__b2 {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(90px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* Teal left, violet right, matching the render's ellipse placement.

     SIZED OFF --measure, NOT IN PIXELS. The render's ellipses are 1200 and 954
     wide on a 1440 frame, which is 1.03 and 0.82 of the 1160 measure this site
     lays out on. Writing those as px would put two more container widths on the
     list tokens.css exists to end, and would also stop the light scaling with
     the page. `min()` against a viewport width keeps the same proportion on a
     phone, where 82% of the measure would be wider than the screen. */
  .hero__b1 {
    width: min(78vw, calc(var(--measure) * 1.03));
    aspect-ratio: 1.9;
    left: -8%;
    top: -46%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    /* --orb-teal is already 0.78 alpha at its core and this disc is roughly
       eight times the area of the ones on the homepage, so the same alpha reads
       eight times brighter. Measured against the render at 1440, the light has
       to come down to about a third for the hero to stay a dark page with light
       on it rather than a coloured page. */
    opacity: 0.34;
  }

  .hero__b2 {
    width: min(62vw, calc(var(--measure) * 0.82));
    aspect-ratio: 1.6;
    right: -4%;
    top: -42%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.26;
  }

  /* ══ ROLE CHIPS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .chips {
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-2);
  }

  .chips__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* --radius-pill and a 10px vertical padding put this well below the card
     scale, so it is a chip by construction rather than by exemption. 44px is
     the WCAG 2.2 target floor, not a style choice. */
  .chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--c-card);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .chip:hover {
    border-color: var(--c-border-glow);
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .chip:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .chip__dot {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* The palette rule, stated once and reused by the artefacts and the trust
     row: teal is a verified fact, orchid is something refused or not held. */
  .chip__dot--verified {
    background: var(--c-teal);
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-teal-glow);
  }

  .chip__dot--refused {
    background: var(--c-orchid);
  }

  /* Centred at the call site, which is where Section.astro says a body that
     needs centring has to ask for it. */
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* ══ THE FOUR ROLE SECTIONS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
     520 / 120 / 480 in the render, on a 1440 frame with 160 margins. Expressed
     as a ratio so it holds at every width the measure resolves to. */
  .role__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.08fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    align-items: center;
  }

  /* Prose stays in source order for a screen reader and for the tab ring; only
     the painted columns swap. Nothing in the artefact card is focusable, so
     there is no reading order to break. */
  .role__grid--mirror .role__text {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .role__grid--mirror .role__art {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .role__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .role__eyebrow--verified { color: var(--c-teal); }
  .role__eyebrow--refused  { color: var(--c-orchid); }

  .role__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h2);
    line-height: 1.14;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .role__p {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* ══ THE ARTEFACT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     `surface surface--pad` in the markup, so the border, radius, glass,
     specular top edge and cast shadow are the one card recipe. Nothing here
     restates any of them. */
  .art {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The mark. In flow rather than absolutely positioned, so it cannot drift
     away from the figure it belongs to at any width. */
  .art::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .art--verified::before {
    background: var(--c-teal);
    box-shadow: 0 0 12px var(--c-teal-glow);
  }

  .art--refused::before {
    background: var(--c-orchid);
  }

  /* --t-h3 rather than the render's ~28px, which is on no tier. The scale has
     four sizes and a fifth opinion is how it starts to drift. */
  .art__figure {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h3);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .art__source {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ══ TABLE STAKES ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .trust {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(16px, 2.4vw, 28px);
  }

  .trust__pair {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .trust__term {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .trust__term::before {
    content: '';
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--c-teal);
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-teal-glow);
  }

  /* The one row that is not a claim. Orchid, unglowed, and it never moves. */
  .trust__card--refused .trust__term::before {
    background: var(--c-orchid);
    box-shadow: none;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  .trust__detail {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ══ MOTION ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Every sequence lives inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, so
     reduced motion is not an override that has to win an argument with the
     cascade: it is zero animations, with every element already painted at its
     final state by the rules above.

     Durations are stated as multiples of --m-beat rather than as milliseconds,
     which is the cadence grammar in tokens.css. 1200ms is 2 beats, 260ms is
     0.44, 60ms is 0.1. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* M-A1 · THE HERO LIGHTS FROM BOTH SIDES
       On load, once. Teal first, violet 0.3 of a beat behind it. The render's
       keyframes show both blooms already at 0.55 of final alpha at 0ms and the
       teal reaching full by 400ms of a 1200ms envelope, which is what
       --m-ease-land does on its own: it spends most of its travel early and
       then settles. No breathing loop and no infinite scale, so nothing on
       this page is still moving once it has arrived. */
    .hero__b1,
    .hero__b2 {
      animation: bloom-in calc(var(--m-beat) * 2) var(--m-ease-land) both;
    }

    .hero__b2 {
      animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.3);
    }

    /* M-A3 · THE FOUR ROLE CHIPS ARRIVE IN READING ORDER
       260ms each, 60ms apart, ease-out, after the standfirst. They are a page
       map rather than the argument, so nothing is lost if the animation never
       runs, and the whole sequence is over in 440ms. */
    .chips__list li .chip {
      animation: chip-in calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.44) ease-out both;
    }

    .chips__list li:nth-child(2) .chip { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.1); }
    .chips__list li:nth-child(3) .chip { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.2); }
    .chips__list li:nth-child(4) .chip { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.3); }
  }

  @keyframes bloom-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0.55;
      transform: scale(0.94);
    }
  }

  /* THE FLOOR IS 0.35, NOT 0. Corrected 2026-08-03, after check-motion.js
     reported this as a live entrance-from-zero.

     With `both` fill and delays running to 0.3 of a beat, the fourth chip sat
     at opacity 0 for the first 440ms of every page load. Not permanently
     hidden, and reduced-motion readers never saw it because the whole block is
     inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference` — but it is the same
     hide-then-reveal shape that failed the axe gate on this codebase before,
     and axe samples DURING an animation.

     0.35 is not a new number: it is the floor styles/motion.css uses for every
     arrival on the site, so the two agree rather than merely coexist.

     STILL DEBT, and recorded as such in the gate: this is a component-local
     entrance, and motion.css now owns arrival. It survives because these chips
     sit above the fold, where the central arrival correctly does nothing, so
     deleting it would remove the sequence rather than centralise it. That is a
     design decision and not a defect fix. */
  @keyframes chip-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0.35;
      transform: translateY(12px);
    }
  }

  /* M-A5 · TRUST DOTS SETTLE, AND ONE DOES NOT CELEBRATE
     The sheet specifies an IntersectionObserver at 25%. This build ships 0 KB
     of JavaScript and a page-scoped observer is the one-off script
     PLATFORM-CHARTER.md forbids, so a named view timeline does the same job in
     CSS. `view-timeline-name` goes on the row and each dot reads it, which is
     what gives the six their stagger: the same animation over six ranges
     offset by 5% of the row's entry.

     THE FALLBACK IS THE FINAL STATE, not the start state, which is the rule
     DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 3 sets for `animation-timeline` while Firefox
     still has it behind a flag. Everything above already paints the settled
     dot; this only takes it away and gives it back.

     The refused dot is excluded by construction rather than by an override. It
     marks the thing we do not hold, and a mark that celebrates its arrival is
     the wrong reading of that. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: --trust-row) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .trust {
        view-timeline-name: --trust-row;
        view-timeline-axis: block;
      }

      .trust__card:not(.trust__card--refused) .trust__term::before {
        animation: dot-settle linear both;
        animation-timeline: --trust-row;
        animation-range: entry 12% entry 52%;
      }

      .trust__card:nth-child(2) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 17% entry 57%; }
      .trust__card:nth-child(3) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 22% entry 62%; }
      .trust__card:nth-child(4) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 27% entry 67%; }
      .trust__card:nth-child(5) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 32% entry 72%; }
    }
  }

  @keyframes dot-settle {
    from {
      opacity: 0.4;
      box-shadow: none;
    }
  }

  /* ══ SHARED BLOCKS, UNCHANGED ═════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .kicker {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ── Two-up cards, used by the provenance pair and the variants ────────── */
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px);
  }

  /* `surface surface--pad` in the markup, not a fourth copy of the recipe. */

  .pair__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
